---
title: Nx plugins
description: Learn what Nx plugins add to your workspace, how a plugin replaces hand-written task configuration, and how to add one with nx add.
sidebar:
  order: 3
filter: 'type:Features'
---

Nx plugins add support for a tool or framework, such as Vite, Jest, Playwright, or Gradle, to your workspace.
Nx runs and caches any script without a plugin.
A plugin adds what Nx can't work out from a script alone, like which files a task reads and where it writes its output.

## Without a plugin

Take a Vite app with a `build` script.
For Nx to cache it correctly, you declare its outputs yourself:

```json
// apps/web/package.json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "vite build"
  },
  "nx": {
    "targets": {
      "build": {
        "outputs": ["{projectRoot}/dist"]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

That `outputs` path duplicates `build.outDir` from `vite.config.ts`.
If you change one and forget the other, Nx caches the wrong folder.
Every Vite project in the workspace needs the same configuration.

## With a plugin

With the `@nx/vite` plugin, you can delete both the script and the target.
The plugin reads each `vite.config.ts` and [infers](/docs/features/run-tasks#inferred-tasks) `build`, `dev`, and `preview` tasks, with outputs taken from `build.outDir`.
When you change the Vite config, the tasks follow it.

Most plugins also ship [generators](/docs/features/generate-code) to scaffold projects or add the tool to an existing one, and migrations that [update the tool and its config](/docs/features/automate-updating-dependencies) when you upgrade.
Some read files Nx doesn't understand on its own.
The `@nx/gradle` plugin, for example, adds Gradle projects and their dependencies to the project graph.

## Add a plugin

Run `nx add` with the plugin package name:

```shell
nx add @nx/vite
```

`nx add` installs the plugin, using the version that matches your Nx version for official `@nx/*` plugins.
It then registers the plugin in `nx.json`:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "plugins": [
    {
      "plugin": "@nx/vite/plugin",
      "options": {
        "buildTargetName": "build",
        "devTargetName": "dev",
        "previewTargetName": "preview"
        // ... other task names
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

The `options` set the names of the inferred tasks.
To see a project's tasks and which plugin or file set each value, run `nx show project <project-name> --web`.

## Find or build a plugin

The Nx team maintains plugins for common frontend, backend, testing, and build tools, and the community publishes plugins for many others.
Browse both in the [plugin registry](/docs/plugin-registry).

If no plugin covers your tool, or your organization has its own conventions, you can [write a plugin](/docs/kb/organization-specific-plugin).
